---
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<figure class="plate">
  <img src={u("/art/gologorica-house-1711.webp")}
       alt="A constructed illustration: a long stone village house of two and three storeys under a tiled roof on projecting brackets, with an arcaded loggia of two round arches reached by open stone steps with a balustrade; on the wall an oval carved cartouche, an empty aedicule with scrolled volutes, and a plain framed stone tablet which is blank. A dry-stone walled lane, a bare fig, hens on the stony ground, and a hill village with a campanile behind under flat grey light."
       width="1400" height="1795" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">
    <strong>The house at Gologorica.</strong> A constructed illustration, made for this archive by an image model working
    from a written brief. It is illustration and not evidence — not a record of the building, not a survey, and not a
    substitute for the photographs the family took in 2026, which are the actual source for everything this site says
    about the house.
    <br /><br />
    <strong>The tablet in it is blank, and that is on purpose.</strong> A first version came back with invented
    pseudo-Latin cut across it, and was withheld. The real tablet is the single most important stone this archive holds,
    read close and settled letter by letter: <span class="mono">ANNO · DOMNI · 1711 / GION · BATISTA / DE · FRANCESCHI /
    FECCE · FARE · PE[R] · SE / ET · HEREDI · SVOI</span> — in the year of the Lord 1711, Gion Batista de Franceschi had
    it made for himself and his heirs. A picture carrying anything else in that panel would be the one thing on this site
    capable of doing real damage, so the panel is empty. The oval cartouche above it is likewise left indistinct: the
    real arms are a cross crossed by two arrows over a pomegranate under a plumed helm, exactly as Carlo described them
    privately to Kobler in about 1873, with a Latin donor's legend running clockwise round the rim — and that carving is
    photographed, drawn and blazoned elsewhere on this site rather than guessed at here.
    <br /><br />
    What the picture is good for is the shape of the thing: a long low house that is the biggest in a poor village, the
    loggia of two arches at one end with its steps up from the lane, the roof on projecting brackets, the dry-stone walls
    and the hens and the flat winter light. The house is number 22. The original file is in this project's source library.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .plate { margin: 0; }
  .plate img { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 620px; height: auto; border-radius: 2px; }
</style>
